Compare realizations under explicit electrical and component limits (@engineering-electrical-limits: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../electrical-power-engineering/foundations/Ratings-operating-states-models-and-evidence.lingua#r_4BA5QEZMPE71TKF40R5EYJ9HMQ") { r_005E639D5F937C2C1C6CEA0834
Use the same load function and screening requirements for three hypothetical realizations. E is in V and all resistances are in Ω; tuple order is (E,R_s,R_c,r₁,r₂,L₁,L₂).

| Design | Parameter tuple | Load voltages | Source current | Useful fraction of ideal-source power |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| A | (12,1,1,2,1,10,5) | both 20/3 V | 2 A | 5/9 |
| B | (12,1/2,1/2,1,1/2,10,5) | both 60/7 V | 18/7 A | 5/7 |
| C | (12,1/2,1/4,1/2,1/4,10,5) | both 160/17 V | 48/17 A | 40/51 |

These are alternative parameter realizations, not purchased products. A fails the lower voltage requirement. B and C meet the nominal voltage/current requirements and their nominal dissipation limits. C reduces the common and branch connection resistances relative to B while retaining the same source resistance. Its source current rises, so source dissipation can rise even while connection losses fall. Replacing every resistance by a smaller number is not a universal optimization rule.

For A, the ideal source supplies 24 W and the two loads receive 40/3 W. For B, useful power is 1080/49 W and ideal-source power 216/7 W. For C, useful power is 7680/289 W and ideal-source power 576/17 W. Independently check every individual loss before quoting the useful fraction. These are exact consequences of the stipulated resistive models, with no statement about a real source's chemical efficiency, capacity or regulation.

Next admit parameter ranges: E may vary ±5% around its nominal value and every resistance ±10%. The full Cartesian box defines allowed combinations; this is an admissible-set statement, not a statistical independence or uniform-distribution assumption. Correlations may narrow the physically possible set. They must be justified rather than silently assumed favorable.

The requirement is now universal over the permitted set. Passing at the nominal point does not prove passing throughout. Conversely, failure of a conservative upper bound does not by itself prove a physical violation; an actual allowed counterexample or a tighter bound is needed. The next lesson proves which extrema lie at corners and uses conservative bounds for powers that need separate care.

**Realization evidence:** a design decision would additionally require actual connection geometry/material, contact and return paths, environmental/duty limits, component tolerances and thermal behavior, inspection and measurement evidence, and operation/maintenance considerations. The present values do not specify those. Follow [Carry a response requirement through realization and inspection](Carry-a-response-requirement-through-realization-and-inspection.lingua#r_00B6F25CACF583F02249520A8B) for the common chain from requirement through realized artifact, and the next shared Record for the distinction between nominal ratings and operating evidence.

**Work:** reconstruct all table entries and list the requirements not covered by this static model. Explain what evidence would support replacing R_c by a lower modeled value. State what new calculation would be needed if resistance changes substantially with temperature. No component or installation is qualified by this table.

**Continue:** [Ratings, operating states, models and evidence](../electrical-power-engineering/foundations/Ratings-operating-states-models-and-evidence.lingua#r_4BA5QEZMPE71TKF40R5EYJ9HMQ).

